WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.006 --> 00:00:16.365
Now I'd like to introduce today's
speakers from right to left.

00:00:16.365 --> 00:00:21.030
Kara Scoggin, who you will be hearing from
later today, is the senior communications

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.770
manager of Stanford Center for
Professional Development, responsible for

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:28.110
advancing student engagement inside and
outside of Stanford.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.520
Today's featured presenters
are Kenji Hakuta, Jeff Zwiers and

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.310
Sara Rutherford-Quach.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:41.150
Sara, a former bilingual elementary
teacher has more than 13 years of

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:45.690
experience working with the linguistically
diverse students and their teachers, and

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:49.200
has conducted extensive research
on instructional practices for

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:49.900
English learners.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.770
Sara was previously awarded
a National Academy of

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.240
Education Spencer Foundation
Dissertation Fellowship for

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.120
her work on the role of silence and

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.600
speech in an elementary classroom
serving language minority students.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.636
Her areas of interests include classroom
discourse and interaction analysis,

00:01:08.636 --> 00:01:14.116
language, culture, and instruction in
multilingual and multicultural educational

00:01:14.116 --> 00:01:20.322
environments, institutional policy and
curricular change and educational equity.

00:01:20.322 --> 00:01:22.887
Sara has been involved with the design and

00:01:22.887 --> 00:01:26.557
teaching of more than 20
MOOC offerings since 2013.

00:01:26.557 --> 00:01:31.463
And she has also directed the development
of many learning modules with the smarter

00:01:31.463 --> 00:01:35.197
balance assessment consortium and
the ELPA21 consortium.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.960
Jeff has worked for more than 15
years as a professional developer and

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.860
instructional mentor in
urban school settings,

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.550
emphasizing the development of literacy,
thinking and academic language for

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.420
linguistically and
culturally diverse students.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.880
He has published books and articles on
reading, thinking, and academic language.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.527
His most recent book is
Academic Conversations: Classroom Talk

00:01:58.527 --> 00:02:02.600
that Fosters Critical Thinking and
Content Understandings.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.638
His current work at the Center to
Support Excellence in Teaching focuses

00:02:07.638 --> 00:02:12.925
on developing teachers, core practices for
teaching academic language,

00:02:12.925 --> 00:02:19.055
comprehension of complex text, and oral
communication skills across subject areas.

00:02:19.055 --> 00:02:24.485
And Kenji Hakuta is active
in education policy.

00:02:24.485 --> 00:02:28.820
He has testified to Congress and courts
on language policy, the education of

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:33.080
language minority students, affirmative
action in higher education, and

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.194
improvement of quality
in educational research.

00:02:36.194 --> 00:02:39.985
Kenji is an elected member of
the National Academy of Education,

00:02:39.985 --> 00:02:43.930
a fellow of the American Educational
Research Association, and

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:49.040
a fellow of the American Association for
the Advancement of Science, recognized for

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.220
his accomplishments in linguistics and
language sciences.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.040
He has served on the board
of various organizations,

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.250
including the Educational Testing Service,
the Spencer Foundation and

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:01.360
the New Teacher Center.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.828
Now I'd like to turn
the floor over to Kenji.

00:03:10.627 --> 00:03:15.014
&gt;&gt; Good morning everyone, and
thank you for taking the time and

00:03:15.014 --> 00:03:19.487
on a nice summer day to join us today for
our presentation about

00:03:19.487 --> 00:03:24.236
the course that we'll be offering,
Rich Talk = Rich Learning.

00:03:24.236 --> 00:03:28.529
And I'm pleased to be opening up
this presentation with my colleagues

00:03:28.529 --> 00:03:31.158
Jeff Zwiers and Sara Rutherford-Quach.

00:03:35.346 --> 00:03:40.000
To begin,
I'd like to view a video of a classroom,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.018
an ELD or an English language development.

00:03:44.018 --> 00:03:48.211
They're in parts of countries called
English as second language or

00:03:48.211 --> 00:03:52.548
it was the new language class,
of fourth grade students who are having

00:03:52.548 --> 00:03:57.100
conversation having just read
The True Story of The Three Little Pigs.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.670
And after reading,

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:03.070
students are discussing as a prompt
whether the wolf was guilty or not.

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:04.760
And so I'm going to play this video.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:12.323
&gt;&gt; The big bad wolf is not guilty
because he was baking a cake for

00:04:12.323 --> 00:04:16.665
his grandma and
he had a really bad cold and

00:04:16.665 --> 00:04:22.768
he blowed the house down by
accident because he had a cold.

00:04:22.768 --> 00:04:27.526
&gt;&gt; So what you're saying is the big
bad wolf isn't guilty because he was

00:04:27.526 --> 00:04:32.037
trying to make a cake for his grandma and
he had a really bad cold and

00:04:32.037 --> 00:04:33.860
he needed sugar to go get.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:39.116
And he accidentally sneezed down
the houses because he had a cold.

00:04:39.116 --> 00:04:43.763
&gt;&gt; Yes, that was what I was saying.

00:04:43.763 --> 00:04:50.461
&gt;&gt; The big bad wolf is guilty because
what if he sneezed on purpose?

00:04:50.461 --> 00:04:54.693
And what if the pigs weren't able
to sneeze it down, I mean to sneeze

00:04:54.693 --> 00:04:59.670
down the house, and he made up his own
story, and he made it all planned?

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:05.623
&gt;&gt; So what you're saying is that
the Big Bad Wolf is guilty,

00:05:05.623 --> 00:05:10.214
because he might have
sneezed on purpose and

00:05:10.214 --> 00:05:13.811
blown the house down on purpose and

00:05:13.811 --> 00:05:19.135
the pigs are [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; What I mean is he's doing

00:05:19.135 --> 00:05:24.193
it on purpose because I
think he made his own

00:05:24.193 --> 00:05:29.260
story to make all the big
bad things align.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:33.848
&gt;&gt; So, you're saying is that
he made up the whole story.

00:05:33.848 --> 00:05:38.158
So, he doesn't want to go to jail.

00:05:38.158 --> 00:05:41.745
&gt;&gt; So now just having watched that video,

00:05:41.745 --> 00:05:46.311
let's just talk about why
the role of rich talk and

00:05:46.311 --> 00:05:51.761
rich learning in this conversation
that we just observed.

00:05:51.761 --> 00:05:56.500
And then ask really questions
about the fundamental

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:01.030
role of language in
classroom as students learn.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:07.531
One is that students
are wired to interact, that

00:06:07.531 --> 00:06:16.340
really the role that language plays
is in facilitating interaction.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:21.020
But what you really saw in the video
was two students who were really

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:26.600
interacting about the prompt of whether
the big bad wolf was guilty or not.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:28.050
Why or why not?

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:29.730
Sharing ideas and so forth.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:33.960
And it's something that you hope
to see in a lot of classrooms and

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:38.850
that's why we showed it and
this is really the purpose of course

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:43.440
is to try to promote
instructional strategies for

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.850
supporting students being able to
engage and interact in this way.

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:50.250
And one of the things we hope
to show you in the video, and

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:56.350
those of you who saw it probably witnessed
this, might think back to the video and

00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:00.300
saying okay, this is really what
language is about for kids.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.800
It's not about repeating vocabulary words.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:08.300
It's not about struggling to find
the correct tense on a verb.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:13.398
It really is about interacting and
engaging with others.

00:07:13.398 --> 00:07:19.294
Another question you might ask of this
video, if you had to think about this was,

00:07:19.294 --> 00:07:23.910
were there opportunities for
students to think on their feet?

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:29.930
A conversation is not about scripted,
going back and forth, reading sentences.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:33.790
But it really is about
spontaneous listening to others,

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.760
thinking about what the other said, and

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:41.830
trying to formulate your own ideas
relative to what the other person said.

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:47.500
And it is a live animal so to speak.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:52.099
It's also an opportunity
to try out ideas that,

00:07:52.099 --> 00:07:57.037
a conversation is wonderful
because not everything

00:07:57.037 --> 00:08:02.198
is transcribed, and
you're not held accountable for

00:08:02.198 --> 00:08:06.390
it except during the immediate moment.

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:08.339
So it's a great time to try out ideas.

00:08:08.339 --> 00:08:09.885
What you think about this?

00:08:09.885 --> 00:08:11.900
Or this is what I'm thinking,
and so forth.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:16.380
So that spontaneity and
trying out ideas is

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:21.210
another important
component of conversations.

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:25.440
Another is around clarifying ideas and
sometimes others will help you,

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:28.750
because they will say not quite
sure what you said there.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:30.580
Or is this what you mean?

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:32.960
And that allows you to

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:36.670
sharpen your own thinking by just by
sort of putting the words out there.

00:08:38.410 --> 00:08:44.400
This conversation is also another way in
which you can try to support your ideas.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.400
Ideas are complex.

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:51.160
They are, whether you're guilty or
not, is a complex

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:55.990
idea and you may find multiple
ways to try to support that.

00:08:55.990 --> 00:09:00.910
And so these are the sorts of things that,
at least those of you who saw the video,

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:03.680
probably saw that
the students struggling with.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:09.410
A lot of this is around language being,
it's a biological thing.

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:15.250
It's something that's alive,
it's spontaneous, it's creative.

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:17.550
And these are the kind of
elements of conversation.

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:18.677
I'm going to ask Sara and

00:09:18.677 --> 00:09:22.689
Jeff if you might want to comment on some
of what you might have seen in the video.

00:09:26.975 --> 00:09:27.475
&gt;&gt; This is Jeff.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:30.520
I'd like to zoom in on clarifying ideas..

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:31.910
What you will see or

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:35.150
what you might have seen in
the video is a lot of paraphrasing.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:42.995
And paraphrasing, even though it seems
like it's just repeating things,

00:09:42.995 --> 00:09:49.680
it's a powerful way for kids to both get
feedback on how clear their ideas are.

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:54.760
But it's also a way to build
language as I clarify your idea,

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.278
or as I paraphrase your idea.

00:09:56.278 --> 00:09:59.780
I'm practicing with the language.

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:04.785
So it's an authentic way
to practice language

00:10:04.785 --> 00:10:10.159
when you're in a conversation with others.

00:10:10.159 --> 00:10:11.985
&gt;&gt; Sara, you want to add anything that.

00:10:11.985 --> 00:10:14.661
They're having a conversation
here by the way.

00:10:14.661 --> 00:10:15.683
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:10:15.683 --> 00:10:17.872
it really is about how we develop
our thinking and develop language.

00:10:17.872 --> 00:10:21.026
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] One thing I wanna add, and
this kind of encompasses all of this.

00:10:21.026 --> 00:10:25.821
Is that all of these things or
even conversation moves or

00:10:25.821 --> 00:10:29.501
are trying to get students to paraphrase.

00:10:29.501 --> 00:10:33.240
You can try these things out but
at the heart of it,

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:39.028
if your classroom does not have a culture
where it's okay to make mistakes and

00:10:39.028 --> 00:10:44.640
it's okay to say things that aren't
exactly the polished final results,

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.660
then a lot of these
things will be difficult.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:51.030
And so one of the things that we're
trying to do in this course and

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:55.700
in this webinar is that we're trying
to build a culture of conversation.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.100
But also a culture where

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.460
Knowledge is something that's
generated throughout time.

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:03.650
And you don't just ask and
answer questions.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.690
You ask a question and
they come back with an answer.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:12.420
So the idea of conversations is that
they're building towards an answer, and

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:13.670
they're working on it.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:17.390
And so that's one of the things
that if that's not there,

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:20.330
then all of these different
points are hard to do.

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:23.270
So I want to make sure that we mention
that as we move on because that's

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:26.495
kind of at the heart of what this building
a culture of classroom conversation is.

00:11:26.495 --> 00:11:28.954
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:11:28.954 --> 00:11:34.684
And this really, the idea of conversation
as being the way in which content,

00:11:34.684 --> 00:11:38.020
thinking and language come together.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.098
Think of them as sort of a macrame or

00:11:40.098 --> 00:11:45.131
blending together of these is really what
we were hoping to promote in this course.

00:11:45.131 --> 00:11:49.772
And the context for this work,
and Sara and Jeff and I and

00:11:49.772 --> 00:11:55.000
others on our team have been working for
the last three years now.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.877
Offering free online courses, MOOCs,

00:11:58.877 --> 00:12:04.220
trying to experimenting
around with this idea.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:08.910
And part of what motivated us to do
this work was because of the shifts in

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:12.490
standards that were going
on around the country.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:17.510
Some of them called our call the Common
Core in English, language arts and math.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:20.590
Many states call them college and
career ready standards.

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:25.120
But all of them are are really
related standards in that

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:30.144
they're really trying to promote deeper,
more rigorous,

00:12:30.144 --> 00:12:36.251
uses of language in the course of
learning English language arts skills,

00:12:36.251 --> 00:12:39.026
as well as mathematical skills.

00:12:39.026 --> 00:12:43.794
And states that have
adopted the next generation

00:12:43.794 --> 00:12:49.450
science standards or
similar variations on science.

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:52.480
A lot of them require
collaborative language.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:56.370
They require argumentation.

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:58.020
They require engagement and

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:03.810
explanation of one's reasoning
around complex texts and so forth.

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:10.190
And so that's what we're trying to gain.

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:16.084
And a lot of the shifts
really require a move from

00:13:16.084 --> 00:13:22.280
thinking about the language
being used as an individual

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:28.770
accumulating of the right answers,
"paying" for points, or playing school.

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:33.554
And rather the actual
engagement with language in

00:13:33.554 --> 00:13:37.700
the course of collaborative
conversations that are happening.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:43.040
And so what we want to see is
less of this kind of formulaic

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:47.890
repetitive kinds of uses of language and
more around collaborating,

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:52.140
understanding, building, using and
communicating whole ideas.

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:56.970
That these new requirements for
how we should really think about

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:03.010
engaging students in rigorous content
that's supported by the shift

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:07.890
in the standards towards
college career ready standards,

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:13.349
really requires this kind of more organic,
more rich,

00:14:13.349 --> 00:14:17.470
collaborative, dyadic or

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:23.520
group conversational uses of language.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:28.660
So I'm going to now
shift this over to Jeff,

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:34.055
who is going to elaborate further on
what we'll be doing in this course.

00:14:34.055 --> 00:14:38.887
&gt;&gt; Jeff here, and I want to start off with

00:14:38.887 --> 00:14:43.569
looking at, there are a lot of fake or

00:14:43.569 --> 00:14:49.150
inauthentic conversations out there.

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:55.190
And here's a nice quotation
by a philosopher I believe.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:58.000
The more genuine conversation is,

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.210
the less it's conduct lies within
the will of either partner...

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:03.510
A conversation has a spirit of its own,
and

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:07.690
the language in which it is conducted
bears its own truth within it.

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:12.020
That is, it allows something him
to emerge which henceforth exists.

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:15.050
So in a conversation,
two people bring their ideas.

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:16.830
But they're not just memorized, and

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:22.120
they're not just one person sharing their
idea with the other, they build something.

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:24.690
And so that's what we're trying
to get out in the classroom,

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:30.090
is how can students work with each other
to construct ideas in conversation.

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:37.447
And, but as Sara eluded to earlier,
we also have to

00:15:37.447 --> 00:15:43.070
foster some conversational
mindsets in our students.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:45.590
It's not just teachers doing things.

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:50.510
It's also students need to unlearn
a few things, and need to learn that

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:56.350
conversation and working with others can
be a powerful way to learn many things.

00:15:56.350 --> 00:16:00.325
And so
here's a semi-humorous slide although,

00:16:00.325 --> 00:16:03.455
there's a non humorous element to it.

00:16:03.455 --> 00:16:07.609
If you look at it, what we'd like you to
just each time with a topic is introduced,

00:16:07.609 --> 00:16:11.120
for example, today will begin our
unit on the American Revolution.

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:17.030
Every student thinks, and this is where
you can start laughing if you want,

00:16:17.030 --> 00:16:19.740
but I can't wait to share my ideas and
hear those of others.

00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:25.110
You know you're probably thinking how many
students actually think that and then.

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:28.180
I'll learn and
remember this better by talking about it.

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:30.800
Now there might be a certain
few kids that might think that,

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:34.580
because they like to talk, but
we're not as concerned about those kids.

00:16:34.580 --> 00:16:37.550
I like it when partners push me to think,
build and clarify.

00:16:38.825 --> 00:16:43.370
Yeah, and I'm not sure how many kids I
know, and I'm open to changing my mind and

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:44.609
learning new ideas from others.

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:50.190
And so these types of mindsets make

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:55.100
up the culture of conversation, and the
culture of learning through conversation.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:57.550
Actually if you look at this,

00:16:57.550 --> 00:17:01.990
I sometimes wonder how many adults
in different settings have these.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:04.940
And what we want,
we want our students to grow up into

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:07.720
adults that also have these
types of mindsets right.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:14.120
That they're not just individual competing
with others and just learning on my own.

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:19.438
And so that's one of the things that
we try to emphasize in our work.

00:17:22.875 --> 00:17:28.534
Let's look at, another area important
area is looking at the prompts,

00:17:28.534 --> 00:17:32.614
this is come up in our ongoing
research that we do with

00:17:32.614 --> 00:17:37.520
teachers related to our online courses and
other work.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:40.815
And we've found that there's a big
struggle to come up with effective

00:17:40.815 --> 00:17:48.170
prompts, and so a couple things
that we'll cover it briefly here.

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:50.330
One is having a purpose for conversing.

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:54.650
A lot of kids and adults don't
often know why they're talking,

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:57.640
don't have a solid purpose or direction.

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:02.640
There's also, the prompt should require
thinking and doing something with ideas.

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:06.950
So, not just regurgitating, but
actually transforming ideas in some way,

00:18:06.950 --> 00:18:10.040
transforming, fortifying,
that kind of thing.

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:13.140
And then finally, they should encourage
students to share novel ideas and

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:16.140
learn from one another,
like we said earlier it's not just

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:21.010
I'm sharing my wisdom with you, and you're
just agreeing and nodding your head.

00:18:21.010 --> 00:18:25.031
So there's a building, there's I bring
some materials to the conversation,

00:18:25.031 --> 00:18:26.591
you bring some materials, and

00:18:26.591 --> 00:18:30.263
then we both construct something that
we couldn't have done on our own.

00:18:30.263 --> 00:18:37.845
And now let's look at some sample
prompts and we'll see how these do.

00:18:37.845 --> 00:18:41.955
What's the most important theme for
fourth graders in this story?

00:18:41.955 --> 00:18:45.415
Does that hit all the boxes here?

00:18:45.415 --> 00:18:49.492
If there is a purpose,
I might have a different idea than you.

00:18:49.492 --> 00:18:53.663
They require thinking, definitely this
type of interpretive thinking, and

00:18:53.663 --> 00:18:55.432
each kid might have a novel idea,

00:18:55.432 --> 00:18:59.613
even if they have the same theme in their
mind as they come to the conversation.

00:18:59.613 --> 00:19:02.491
There's different reasons for it and
different ways of expressing it.

00:19:02.491 --> 00:19:06.614
Often there's going to
be different evidence.

00:19:06.614 --> 00:19:08.365
How about.

00:19:12.394 --> 00:19:16.626
What conclusions can we make about animal
adaptations based on this evidence?

00:19:16.626 --> 00:19:18.100
So it's a science prompt.

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:19.517
Does it have a purpose?

00:19:19.517 --> 00:19:20.237
Yes.

00:19:20.237 --> 00:19:21.258
Does it require thinking?

00:19:21.258 --> 00:19:21.874
Yes.

00:19:21.874 --> 00:19:23.987
Does it encourage students
to share novel ideas?

00:19:23.987 --> 00:19:28.206
In most cases, students will be
able to share novel ideas and

00:19:28.206 --> 00:19:31.359
have them synthesize and combine together.

00:19:31.359 --> 00:19:37.009
How about, collaborate to create
a real world word problem

00:19:37.009 --> 00:19:42.552
that requires both adding and
subtracting of fractions.

00:19:42.552 --> 00:19:46.152
So this also, there's a purpose,
they're constructing something,

00:19:46.152 --> 00:19:50.341
it requires thinking, because they have to
apply some of the thinking that they've

00:19:50.341 --> 00:19:52.778
been doing in other
probably similar problems.

00:19:52.778 --> 00:19:57.387
And it encourages them to share some novel
ideas about what kind of problem we're

00:19:57.387 --> 00:19:58.828
gonna create together.

00:19:58.828 --> 00:20:02.636
And then finally,
another example, would be.

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.688
Was Christopher Columbus a hero or not?

00:20:06.688 --> 00:20:10.806
And this is a common topic
in a lot of schools and

00:20:10.806 --> 00:20:15.570
what I like to do is,
does this does it have a purpose?

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:16.080
Yes.

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:17.033
Does it require thinking?

00:20:17.033 --> 00:20:19.761
And does it encourage students
to share novel ideas?

00:20:19.761 --> 00:20:23.853
And what I'd like to do is
look at again look at these.

00:20:23.853 --> 00:20:27.886
Let's see if the prompt actually
works with this little snippet of

00:20:27.886 --> 00:20:28.975
a conversation.

00:20:28.975 --> 00:20:30.334
Okay, so it's the same prompt.

00:20:30.334 --> 00:20:32.110
I don't think Columbus was a hero.

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:33.665
Why not?

00:20:33.665 --> 00:20:37.311
Cuz he hurt them and
made them to be slaves.

00:20:37.311 --> 00:20:41.884
But he helped Spain get rich and
he found America.

00:20:41.884 --> 00:20:43.058
So?

00:20:43.058 --> 00:20:43.917
He hurt them.

00:20:43.917 --> 00:20:46.048
Heroes don't hurt or make slaves.

00:20:46.048 --> 00:20:46.871
Maybe.

00:20:46.871 --> 00:20:49.066
But he was brave to keep sailing.

00:20:49.066 --> 00:20:50.703
He had maps.

00:20:50.703 --> 00:20:52.270
And he met a Portuguese guy.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:55.323
That guy should be famous, not Columbus.

00:20:55.323 --> 00:20:59.214
Okay, so did the prompt work?

00:20:59.214 --> 00:21:02.074
And that's one of the questions
that we wanna continue to ask as we

00:21:02.074 --> 00:21:03.277
work with conversations.

00:21:03.277 --> 00:21:07.491
The conversations are extremely messy,
especially paired conversations.

00:21:07.491 --> 00:21:12.774
You have 15 to whatever conversations
going on simultaneously.

00:21:12.774 --> 00:21:14.260
It's a messy business.

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:19.032
It's loud and you can only listen to a few
at a time when you're in the classroom.

00:21:19.032 --> 00:21:22.376
So, we have to really
get our prompts right.

00:21:22.376 --> 00:21:24.721
And, that's one of the things
that we try to work on.

00:21:24.721 --> 00:21:26.440
How can we come up with prompts?

00:21:26.440 --> 00:21:29.516
Of course, there's a lot that
leads into that prompt as well.

00:21:29.516 --> 00:21:33.092
There's a lot of things that kids need
to learn, like about the Portuguese guy.

00:21:33.092 --> 00:21:37.411
But, we want kids to learn things and
then be able to share them in these

00:21:37.411 --> 00:21:42.539
conversations, because a lot, it's almost
like a foundry where they're adding

00:21:42.539 --> 00:21:47.228
things and they're coming up with new
things, or adding their own ideas and

00:21:47.228 --> 00:21:51.797
then they're coming up with new things
in this foundry of conversation.

00:21:51.797 --> 00:21:56.790
And I think, I'm gonna pass it on now.

00:21:56.790 --> 00:22:01.024
We're gonna dig in even more deeply to
look at the formative assessment of

00:22:01.024 --> 00:22:01.990
conversation.

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:06.182
I'm gonna pass it on to Sara now.

00:22:06.182 --> 00:22:11.259
&gt;&gt; Okay, so this is a great segue because
we talked a little bit about prompts and

00:22:11.259 --> 00:22:16.025
how forming a thoughtful prompts that
engenders conversation is really

00:22:16.025 --> 00:22:16.898
important.

00:22:16.898 --> 00:22:20.025
And not necessarily
a problem that just ask for

00:22:20.025 --> 00:22:22.681
a single answer or a series of answers.

00:22:22.681 --> 00:22:27.326
But that is important,
but only when paired with

00:22:27.326 --> 00:22:31.530
a formative assessment of conversations.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:33.210
And what we mean by that

00:22:34.250 --> 00:22:38.180
is basically listening to
current student conversations.

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:41.580
Where your students are right now.

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:44.960
Or if it's a summer,
where other students are right now.

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:49.990
And then thinking about,
where you'd like them to be?

00:22:49.990 --> 00:22:55.380
So a lot of you and when I was
the teacher I did turn in talks, right?

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:57.700
So I taught kindergarten and
first grade, and

00:22:57.700 --> 00:23:01.520
I would have the kids turn to each other
and talk all the time, probably two or

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:05.280
three, four times on the carpet
while we were there.

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:09.130
But I didn't necessarily know
what they were all saying and

00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:13.830
I couldn't, because there was 1 of me and
20 different kids, so there's 10 different

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:18.200
conversations going on, or I hope there's
10 different conversations going on.

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:22.710
So one thing that we really advocate for
is really focusing in on one or

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:27.700
two conversation, recording those
conversations and listening to

00:23:27.700 --> 00:23:32.640
the recording and transcribing it so that
you can really tell what kids are saying.

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:35.910
So one thing we've learned from that
is a lot of times when we think

00:23:35.910 --> 00:23:40.150
kids are saying a certain thing we think
students are conversing in a certain way

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:43.410
even when we're hearing them,
when we record the conversations and

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:46.810
we listen back to it,
that's not actually what happened.

00:23:46.810 --> 00:23:51.721
And so actually listening and paying
very close attention to where students

00:23:51.721 --> 00:23:55.787
are currently in their conversations
is really important, so

00:23:55.787 --> 00:23:59.330
that you can get them to where
you would like them to be.

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:01.334
And we've created a tool.

00:24:04.104 --> 00:24:08.277
If I can get this called the conversation
analysis tool that will help you formally

00:24:08.277 --> 00:24:09.680
assess the conversation.

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:15.140
And this, as you see here on the fly,
this dimension zero, dimension one and

00:24:15.140 --> 00:24:15.660
dimension two.

00:24:15.660 --> 00:24:18.335
So I'm going to talk about dimension
one and dimension two first,

00:24:18.335 --> 00:24:21.730
because these are the first two
dimensions that we've created.

00:24:21.730 --> 00:24:25.370
So there's only two two
main dimensions here.

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:28.030
And we've kept it simple on
purpose because we want you to be

00:24:28.030 --> 00:24:30.430
very focused on these things, and

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:34.330
we found them to be the most important and
building up a conversation.

00:24:34.330 --> 00:24:38.870
The dimension one, turns build and
previous turns to build up an idea, and

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:41.828
by turn,
we mean when one person is talking.

00:24:41.828 --> 00:24:42.990
So if Jeff and

00:24:42.990 --> 00:24:48.170
I were having a conversation right now,
I would say hey, Jeff, how are you doing?

00:24:48.170 --> 00:24:51.480
And that would be one turn,
and Jeff may answer me and

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:52.790
then that would be a second turn.

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:54.310
Now it's his turn.

00:24:54.310 --> 00:24:57.120
So dimension one, we say Jeff and

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:01.520
I turn we build on each other, so
that we build up an idea over time.

00:25:01.520 --> 00:25:05.730
So say our question was
a prompt that we had before.

00:25:05.730 --> 00:25:07.480
It was Columbus a hero.

00:25:07.480 --> 00:25:10.760
And so, I may say I don't
think Columbus is a hero,

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:13.110
and just may ask me why
do you not think that?

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:17.010
But it's not unless it's over time
to build up an idea about whether

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:18.970
Columbus is or is not a hero.

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:22.300
And then so that's the first one,

00:25:22.300 --> 00:25:27.410
build up on turns build on each others
build up an idea, and then dimension two,

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:31.630
it turns focus on the content or
skills related to the lessons objectives.

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:34.620
So this is not anything new for educators.

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:37.600
Basically, we want to make sure
that students are actually talking

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:38.954
about the problems.

00:25:38.954 --> 00:25:41.938
They're addressing the prompts.

00:25:41.938 --> 00:25:44.700
As an early elementary teacher,
this would happen a lot,

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:49.910
we'd ask a question about so
what did Tommy do at that birthday party?

00:25:49.910 --> 00:25:53.410
And the kids would go off, and I had a
birthday party last week, and I got cake.

00:25:53.410 --> 00:25:57.370
And so, that's the sort of you know
in the realm of birthday parties, but

00:25:57.370 --> 00:26:00.800
it's not actually addressing
the prompt the dimension two is,

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:03.070
are they actually addressing that?

00:26:03.070 --> 00:26:07.880
And one thing that the teachers that
we worked with in the past have

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:12.260
found is when we're focusing on that,
a lot of times we have a ha ha's, and

00:26:12.260 --> 00:26:16.810
realize sometimes that our problems that
we're crafting don't necessarily match

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:18.690
the objectives that we had.

00:26:18.690 --> 00:26:23.650
So by looking very closely at this,
you can see where is the match between

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:26.990
the prompt and the objective and
the students conversation.

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:29.650
That's a dimension one and dimension two.

00:26:29.650 --> 00:26:34.540
So, dimension zero is students
take appropriate turns.

00:26:34.540 --> 00:26:38.680
Now, we added this dimension, this is
an optional dimension, but we added it in

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:43.150
here, after getting feedback from
specifically early elementary teachers,

00:26:43.150 --> 00:26:47.290
kindergarten, first, second graders,
pre-K teachers who were saying

00:26:47.290 --> 00:26:49.540
these dimensions are great, but
my students aren't there yet,

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:53.050
they're still practicing how to
actually take turns with each other.

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:57.440
And so, let's say, and
I have two young children, so

00:26:57.440 --> 00:26:59.830
I can see this in practice as well.

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:02.340
So, I take a turn, you take a turn.

00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:05.665
And so, a lot of times, particular
when you're asking them to address

00:27:05.665 --> 00:27:09.805
particular academic subject for
a prompt, kids may talk over each other,

00:27:10.855 --> 00:27:15.755
one person may talk and do all of the
talking, the other person doesn't respond.

00:27:15.755 --> 00:27:19.175
So, dimensions here are basically looks
at whether or not they're taking turns.

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:23.622
So this is a conversation analysis tool.

00:27:23.622 --> 00:27:28.700
We are going to keep that in mind now,
we're just going to focus on,

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:32.080
we'll do all three dimensions, dimension
zero, dimension one and dimension two.

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:37.460
And let's take a look at
some sample conversations.

00:27:37.460 --> 00:27:40.650
And I'm going to ask my colleagues,
Jeff and Kenji, to join me here,

00:27:40.650 --> 00:27:42.400
where we're going to look
at two conversations,

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:46.510
one from a fourth grade class, and
one from, I think a first grade class.

00:27:46.510 --> 00:27:48.520
This is the first grade one first.

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:53.110
And we're going to go back and we're
going to look at the conversation, and

00:27:53.110 --> 00:27:55.550
then we're going to try
to formatively assess it,

00:27:55.550 --> 00:27:59.360
based on the conversation
now with this tool.

00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:01.300
Just to give you an idea of how it works.

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:03.230
So maybe Jeff,
can you read this conversation with me?

00:28:03.230 --> 00:28:03.730
I'll be A.

00:28:06.210 --> 00:28:07.430
And we don't know the prompt.

00:28:07.430 --> 00:28:09.010
Do you know the prompt?

00:28:09.010 --> 00:28:13.580
&gt;&gt; I think it should, the main character,
but share the bread at the end or not?

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:15.990
&gt;&gt; Okay, and
this is about the little red hen, right?

00:28:15.990 --> 00:28:17.620
Okay.
&gt;&gt; And just before that,

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:19.630
a quick little quiz.

00:28:19.630 --> 00:28:24.060
What were the dimensions again
of the conversation else?

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:27.838
Because as you as we read this,
probably not in front of you right now,

00:28:27.838 --> 00:28:29.320
what was the mention zero?

00:28:31.962 --> 00:28:32.874
Can you remember?
&gt;&gt; Can I answer, Jeff?

00:28:32.874 --> 00:28:35.992
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:35.992 --> 00:28:40.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Dimensions zero is
are the students taking turns.

00:28:40.460 --> 00:28:42.690
&gt;&gt; Mention one.

00:28:42.690 --> 00:28:46.440
&gt;&gt; Kenji.
&gt;&gt; Did they build on each other?

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:48.150
&gt;&gt; To build up an idea.

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:49.680
&gt;&gt; Built up an idea.

00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:56.402
&gt;&gt; Did the turns address the lessons
objective, and that it's not written there

00:28:56.402 --> 00:28:59.579
in the prompt, but
basically the objectives of the content.

00:28:59.579 --> 00:29:01.787
&gt;&gt; Focus on the content, okay, good.

00:29:01.787 --> 00:29:05.300
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:29:05.300 --> 00:29:06.530
&gt;&gt; Okay, and this is a little red hen.

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:13.574
Okay.
I think she not share it.

00:29:15.729 --> 00:29:17.185
&gt;&gt; Why?

00:29:17.185 --> 00:29:18.340
&gt;&gt; Because the other animals not work.

00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:19.000
They're just lazy.

00:29:20.470 --> 00:29:23.420
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
I don't think to share, too.

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:23.920
&gt;&gt; Why?

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:25.700
&gt;&gt; Like my dad.

00:29:25.700 --> 00:29:26.580
He work for money.

00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:27.536
Not work, no money.

00:29:27.536 --> 00:29:31.730
Yeah, no [FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; Not fair,

00:29:31.730 --> 00:29:35.350
but teacher said it's good to share.

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:37.390
&gt;&gt; I share when they help.

00:29:37.390 --> 00:29:41.668
Maybe I paint the school,
it's my money, you know?

00:29:41.668 --> 00:29:43.663
&gt;&gt; No.
Maybe if [FOREIGN] like they're

00:29:43.663 --> 00:29:44.590
sick, maybe.

00:29:44.590 --> 00:29:46.740
But this story, animals are lazy.

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:52.898
&gt;&gt; It's good to share, but
not all the time, not with the lazy.

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:56.850
Okay, so you can see.

00:29:56.850 --> 00:29:58.140
&gt;&gt; They did take turns.

00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:06.560
&gt;&gt; So, and we didn't show you
basically their different categories.

00:30:06.560 --> 00:30:12.850
If there's no evidence of taking turns,
then you might say there's no evidence,

00:30:12.850 --> 00:30:15.770
if there's strong evidence, and
you'd say there's strong evidence.

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:18.530
A couple things that we want to
stress before we actually look at

00:30:18.530 --> 00:30:22.870
dimensions one and two, the conversation
on the stool is not a grading tool.

00:30:22.870 --> 00:30:26.440
It's not a summative tool,
it's basically for formative assessment.

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:29.360
It's to help you see where your
students are so you can get there, but

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:32.290
it's not for students to get a grade,
it's for you and for

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:35.460
them to figure out how you
can help them move forward.

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:39.220
The second thing is the conversation
else this tool is for two people.

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:41.580
It's not for one person.

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:43.540
So I'm not going to go to student A and

00:30:43.540 --> 00:30:46.630
say student A did a great job
of taking turns, they did great.

00:30:46.630 --> 00:30:49.740
It's them together as a unit, and

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:53.380
that's because conversations can
help happen with one person.

00:30:53.380 --> 00:30:55.812
And we also want to, we try to, to really,

00:30:55.812 --> 00:30:59.057
really advocate for
not using this as a grading tool, and

00:30:59.057 --> 00:31:03.810
it's harder to use it as a grading tool if
you're using two people rather than one.

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:08.906
Okay, that's my spiel, so you think
Kenji that they were taking turns?

00:31:08.906 --> 00:31:11.403
&gt;&gt; Well, they did they turns.

00:31:11.403 --> 00:31:13.980
&gt;&gt; Yep, okay, what about to mention one?

00:31:15.870 --> 00:31:19.200
Dimension one is turned to build on
each other in order to build up an idea.

00:31:25.200 --> 00:31:27.210
&gt;&gt; I would agree that they
weren't just popcorn.

00:31:27.210 --> 00:31:31.080
The ideas around and in terms,
definitely connected to one another.

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:37.674
And an idea was built up at the end, I
think the idea of not sharing with lazies.

00:31:37.674 --> 00:31:41.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Let's remember this
is a little red hen, right?

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:47.960
And so, if we had the tool in front of us
that had the more detailed dimensions, and

00:31:47.960 --> 00:31:51.950
the different categories of
each dimension, no evidence

00:31:51.950 --> 00:31:56.580
all the way up to strong evidence,
this may be somewhere in between.

00:31:56.580 --> 00:32:00.940
Strong evidence and the category
that's a little bit below that.

00:32:00.940 --> 00:32:01.570
Yeah.

00:32:05.310 --> 00:32:09.598
And one more thing is that these are first
graders who are bilingual students who

00:32:09.598 --> 00:32:11.654
their English is starting to emerge.

00:32:11.654 --> 00:32:15.893
And one thing we want to point out is you
can see there's a lot of quote unquote

00:32:15.893 --> 00:32:17.558
grammatical errors in this.

00:32:17.558 --> 00:32:20.541
That does not matter for
the conversation analysis tool.

00:32:20.541 --> 00:32:25.325
We are not measuring whether they have
again, quote and quote, perfect English.

00:32:25.325 --> 00:32:30.092
Because what we're measuring is whether or
not they're building a each other and

00:32:30.092 --> 00:32:33.893
building up a conversation and
building up knowledge together,

00:32:33.893 --> 00:32:37.569
which doesn't require,
total acquisition of the language.

00:32:39.990 --> 00:32:42.851
And this is the fourth
grade science lesson.

00:32:42.851 --> 00:32:46.350
And Jeff, can you give us a prompt for
this one?

00:32:46.350 --> 00:32:48.090
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's how do animals evolve.

00:32:48.090 --> 00:32:50.840
&gt;&gt; There you go, it's right there, okay.

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:51.642
&gt;&gt; Terms on the board.

00:32:51.642 --> 00:32:53.766
&gt;&gt; Okay, so I'll be Elia.

00:32:53.766 --> 00:32:54.416
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:32:54.416 --> 00:32:56.285
&gt;&gt; So how do animals evolve?

00:32:56.285 --> 00:32:57.142
&gt;&gt; To live.

00:32:57.142 --> 00:32:58.796
&gt;&gt; What's an example?

00:32:58.796 --> 00:33:00.143
&gt;&gt; Sharks.

00:33:00.143 --> 00:33:02.999
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] What's another example?

00:33:02.999 --> 00:33:04.043
&gt;&gt; Turtles.

00:33:04.043 --> 00:33:04.543
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] What's another example?

00:33:07.990 --> 00:33:08.610
&gt;&gt; Giraffes.

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:10.521
They got long necks.

00:33:10.521 --> 00:33:14.078
&gt;&gt; Why?
&gt;&gt; To reach up into trees.

00:33:14.078 --> 00:33:15.693
&gt;&gt; Okay, let's write these down.

00:33:18.874 --> 00:33:20.780
So let's do a quick assessment of that.

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:22.628
How about dimension zero
were they taking turns?

00:33:26.367 --> 00:33:27.284
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Sort of.

00:33:27.284 --> 00:33:28.248
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:33:28.248 --> 00:33:32.323
&gt;&gt; Right it goes student A,
student B, Elia and then Alex.

00:33:32.323 --> 00:33:36.288
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there definitely is the turn
taking, and then you get to dimension one

00:33:36.288 --> 00:33:40.460
then you start talking about well,
are they really building up something.

00:33:40.460 --> 00:33:43.884
They are building on one another's ideas,
sort of.

00:33:43.884 --> 00:33:47.998
But they're not really
building up anything yet.

00:33:47.998 --> 00:33:52.131
&gt;&gt; Yep, I mean they're not
randomly saying things, right?

00:33:52.131 --> 00:33:55.490
But they're not building
an up idea together.

00:33:55.490 --> 00:33:56.670
They're more making a list.

00:33:56.670 --> 00:33:59.379
&gt;&gt; There's a little potential at the end
with that they got long necks to reach up

00:33:59.379 --> 00:34:00.180
in the trees, right?

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:00.935
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah.

00:34:00.935 --> 00:34:04.405
&gt;&gt; So they're kinda getting there,
and this is an important point.

00:34:04.405 --> 00:34:08.485
Sometimes it does take a while in
a conversation, for kids to actually get

00:34:08.485 --> 00:34:12.755
to as an adults, to actually get
to yeah we are building something.

00:34:12.755 --> 00:34:15.345
There are some ideas we can talk about,
right?

00:34:15.345 --> 00:34:16.999
So there is a little warm up.

00:34:16.999 --> 00:34:20.993
&gt;&gt; In this conversation little bit
models what the teachers might be asking

00:34:20.993 --> 00:34:21.980
the whole class.

00:34:21.980 --> 00:34:27.418
A series of questions, and then answers,
rotating around the students.

00:34:27.418 --> 00:34:32.707
&gt;&gt; And yeah, we do want students to become
more like teachers in some respects.

00:34:32.707 --> 00:34:37.145
If you look at Alex's to live now
wouldn't enough for a teacher.

00:34:37.145 --> 00:34:41.515
A teacher who was listening to that
answer, a teacher would say okay,

00:34:41.515 --> 00:34:44.490
well let me explain what that means.

00:34:44.490 --> 00:34:46.640
And also with the sharks example.

00:34:46.640 --> 00:34:48.808
A teacher wouldn't say
what's another example,

00:34:48.808 --> 00:34:50.540
a teacher would say you elaborate.

00:34:50.540 --> 00:34:53.477
Please elaborate how sharks
answers its question.

00:34:53.477 --> 00:34:56.645
So there would be more
pushing from the teacher,

00:34:56.645 --> 00:34:59.050
which is what we want students to do.

00:34:59.050 --> 00:35:04.120
We want students to be like little mini
teachers out in the classroom that elicit

00:35:04.120 --> 00:35:10.450
lots more language out of students than
what normally happens between students.

00:35:10.450 --> 00:35:14.070
&gt;&gt; Okay, so
dimension one as far building up.

00:35:14.070 --> 00:35:17.020
So our consensus is that we don't really
feel like there's a lot of building up

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:23.040
here, a little bit of potential at the
bottom, but not a strong evidence of it.

00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:27.535
What about dimension two, is do the turns
address the content or the objective?

00:35:33.352 --> 00:35:36.116
So the prompt again was, so
how do the animals evolve?

00:35:41.549 --> 00:35:43.987
&gt;&gt; I mean it's not way off base,
they have some examples.

00:35:43.987 --> 00:35:47.698
In their head, I think they do have,
they're not explaining it,

00:35:47.698 --> 00:35:49.180
which is what we want.

00:35:49.180 --> 00:35:49.720
&gt;&gt; Exactly.
&gt;&gt; But

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:53.000
I think they’re definitely getting there.

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:57.760
So I would say wherever that
level would be some evidence.

00:35:57.760 --> 00:35:59.000
&gt;&gt; In the middle, yeah, some evidence.

00:36:00.510 --> 00:36:01.598
&gt;&gt; Lots to work with.

00:36:01.598 --> 00:36:05.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So
another thing we want to point out is

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:10.660
that the syntax of these examples, the
fourth grade example, is perfect right?

00:36:10.660 --> 00:36:12.610
The questions are articulated,

00:36:12.610 --> 00:36:16.577
in a way that would be stereotypically
grammatically correct, but

00:36:16.577 --> 00:36:20.415
that does not matter because this
actually shows more evidence.

00:36:20.415 --> 00:36:24.649
The first grade example shows more
evidence of building on each other to

00:36:24.649 --> 00:36:29.330
build up an idea and conversation
than the fourth grade example does.

00:36:29.330 --> 00:36:32.140
So this is how this tool is a little
bit different than things that you

00:36:32.140 --> 00:36:33.060
may have used in the past.

00:36:35.042 --> 00:36:41.410
Okay so I think this is our last
slide before we open it up a bit.

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:44.900
And so as we move forward and
as you move forward for preparing for

00:36:44.900 --> 00:36:48.620
formative assessment of conversations
in your classrooms, come this fall.

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:52.630
There are a few things that
you can do to get ready for

00:36:52.630 --> 00:36:54.320
this, if you take on this challenge.

00:36:54.320 --> 00:36:57.080
The first thing is look at
the big ideas in each unit to

00:36:57.080 --> 00:36:59.355
draft conversation prompts.

00:36:59.355 --> 00:37:02.560
I know one thing that I was guilty
of as it is a classroom teacher,

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:06.150
I would wait to get right in front of
the students to think about prompts and

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:09.975
then I would ask them to turn and talk,
and I would just pose a question.

00:37:09.975 --> 00:37:12.030
But I wouldn't really
put a lot of time and

00:37:12.030 --> 00:37:14.430
energy into developing those
prompts ahead of time.

00:37:14.430 --> 00:37:18.210
So other times my on the on
the fly questions didn't actually

00:37:18.210 --> 00:37:20.310
engender the type of
conversations I was looking for.

00:37:20.310 --> 00:37:23.900
So that's one, and
then here's two and three.

00:37:23.900 --> 00:37:25.720
Think about what you want to see.

00:37:25.720 --> 00:37:27.970
So if you were asking
a question about Columbus,

00:37:27.970 --> 00:37:32.710
what sort of conversations would you
want to see in an ideal situation?

00:37:32.710 --> 00:37:34.260
And then write those down.

00:37:34.260 --> 00:37:38.720
It actually helps for
you to envision what the conversations or

00:37:38.720 --> 00:37:40.730
the model conversations would be.

00:37:40.730 --> 00:37:45.250
And then I would say, when time comes,
share those with the students, too.

00:37:45.250 --> 00:37:48.000
Number three is practice with
the conversation analysis tool.

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:52.340
So that's the tool that we
just demonstrated with.

00:37:52.340 --> 00:37:53.870
It's called the conversation
analysis tool.

00:37:53.870 --> 00:37:58.280
It has two required dimensions and
one optional dimension.

00:37:58.280 --> 00:38:00.500
And you won't be able to practice with
your own students because they're

00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:03.930
obviously you're not in school right
now unless you're in year around.

00:38:03.930 --> 00:38:07.383
But we have sample conversations that
you can practice with until then.

00:38:07.383 --> 00:38:11.460
And then four, write model
conversations that show features.

00:38:11.460 --> 00:38:14.744
This is an extension of number two, right,
thinking about what you want to see and

00:38:14.744 --> 00:38:16.090
then you actually write it down.

00:38:16.090 --> 00:38:20.330
And number five, start assessing
the first month of school and

00:38:20.330 --> 00:38:23.160
have students self- and peer-assess.

00:38:23.160 --> 00:38:26.230
And so again,
this is start formatively assessing, or

00:38:26.230 --> 00:38:29.490
start the process of formatively assessing
conversations during the first months of

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:32.170
school and
involve students in that process.

00:38:32.170 --> 00:38:37.250
So look at model conversations together,
record their own conversations,

00:38:37.250 --> 00:38:39.900
have the students listen
to their own conversations.

00:38:39.900 --> 00:38:44.797
And then they can have an idea of
what you're looking for and so

00:38:44.797 --> 00:38:47.163
they can move closer to that.

00:38:47.163 --> 00:38:51.346
Okay, and then I'm wanna open up
to final thoughts from Jeff and

00:38:51.346 --> 00:38:55.233
Kenji before we address any
questions that are lingering.

00:39:00.817 --> 00:39:05.180
&gt;&gt; Well that was a lot in
a little compact space.

00:39:07.160 --> 00:39:08.712
We hope that it has given you, and

00:39:08.712 --> 00:39:11.216
we will probably answer some
questions here as well.

00:39:11.216 --> 00:39:16.976
But we hope that it's given you,
I'd say some inspiration and

00:39:16.976 --> 00:39:21.620
some interest in trying
some of these things out.

00:39:21.620 --> 00:39:27.911
Because one of the things that we've
also encountered is, it takes time and

00:39:27.911 --> 00:39:34.022
patience, and lots of trials to get
these things going in the classroom.

00:39:34.022 --> 00:39:38.566
If you just try it once twice even for
a month or two,

00:39:38.566 --> 00:39:43.230
it doesn't really get going
till maybe half a year.

00:39:43.230 --> 00:39:48.064
Some teachers will say no, it took me
a year or two to actually understand

00:39:48.064 --> 00:39:52.697
how to get kids conversing and
how to build their conversation skills.

00:39:52.697 --> 00:39:57.363
So it's really,
the question often comes up is this

00:39:57.363 --> 00:40:00.767
worth the time, is it worth the time?

00:40:00.767 --> 00:40:05.946
Most people on this webinar
are probably interested in it enough to

00:40:05.946 --> 00:40:11.584
think it's worth the time but
in a larger swath of district and schools,

00:40:11.584 --> 00:40:16.946
we're still doing the kind of this
traditional memorize the stuff,

00:40:16.946 --> 00:40:21.589
take a test, pay for
my points with my facts and that's it.

00:40:21.589 --> 00:40:24.509
And there's a lot of the traditional and
so this

00:40:24.509 --> 00:40:29.473
is a major overhaul as Kenji alluded to in
the beginning and so thinking about it,

00:40:29.473 --> 00:40:34.250
we have to think about and
really listen in to kids conversations.

00:40:34.250 --> 00:40:38.034
Valuing what they have to say,
valuing their voices but

00:40:38.034 --> 00:40:43.024
also at the same time thinking how is
this useful for learning of content,

00:40:43.024 --> 00:40:47.956
learning of language, learning of
thinking skills and social skills.

00:40:47.956 --> 00:40:49.210
One of those things is often missing.

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:55.835
A lot of kids it's just the ability to
have good conversations with a wide

00:40:55.835 --> 00:40:57.059
range of others.

00:40:57.059 --> 00:41:01.181
So I'm hoping that some
of the stuff will come

00:41:01.181 --> 00:41:04.988
out in the future as we work through this.

00:41:04.988 --> 00:41:10.430
&gt;&gt; I'd like you to share that during
the past few years of doing this work,

00:41:10.430 --> 00:41:15.930
we've heard from lots of districts
who have been using these tools, and

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:21.090
that there is tremendous variation
out there in how much the orientation

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:27.950
towards conversation as a form
of academic learning is sort of

00:41:27.950 --> 00:41:32.910
integrated or becoming sort of
a district-wide as a culture.

00:41:32.910 --> 00:41:37.930
And for some, you see it more in
certain subject areas than for others.

00:41:37.930 --> 00:41:40.060
English language arts more than math.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:44.720
Others you see more of it and so
there are these pockets of kind of

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:49.620
cultural changes for a culture of
conversation that's happening.

00:41:49.620 --> 00:41:54.870
But I just want to acknowledge that
many of you will be in districts with,

00:41:54.870 --> 00:41:57.295
in different kinds of settings.

00:41:57.295 --> 00:42:02.445
The other thing I would like to share
is just that we kind of started this

00:42:02.445 --> 00:42:07.790
workout thinking about supporting
English language learners.

00:42:07.790 --> 00:42:12.860
But as we've done this work and
seen the responses to it, a lot

00:42:12.860 --> 00:42:17.300
of the realization is that this really is,
you might think of English language

00:42:17.300 --> 00:42:22.750
learners as being the tip of the iceberg
or the canary in the mine or whatever.

00:42:22.750 --> 00:42:24.050
And these are students for

00:42:24.050 --> 00:42:28.940
whom this kind of engagement with language
is necessary for English language

00:42:28.940 --> 00:42:33.820
development but it's really a form
of learning for all students.

00:42:33.820 --> 00:42:37.930
And so the uptake of our work is then
not just about teachers who would

00:42:37.930 --> 00:42:42.090
consider themselves English language
learners but for all teachers

00:42:42.090 --> 00:42:46.318
who think of themselves as teachers
primarily of core academic content.

00:42:46.318 --> 00:42:48.165
&gt;&gt; Yep.

