A portion.

00:44:21.690 --> 00:44:25.410
We have some wonderful question
from our participants online,

00:44:25.410 --> 00:44:29.805
the first one being how did students react
when they know they're being recorded?.

00:44:29.805 --> 00:44:33.160
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:43.804 --> 00:44:46.296
&gt;&gt; I'm muted, sorry.

00:44:46.296 --> 00:44:49.066
Okay, I'm not muted now, right?
Okay, so this is Sara and I was gonna say

00:44:49.066 --> 00:44:54.083
it takes a little bit of time for students
to feel comfortable recorded but we've

00:44:54.083 --> 00:44:58.802
done it in our classrooms for forever and
I would say two or three days of doing

00:44:58.802 --> 00:45:03.870
this depending on a student's age and
they just act like nothing's happening.

00:45:03.870 --> 00:45:07.947
We have usually typically
used audio recording and

00:45:07.947 --> 00:45:12.116
in our previous courses
that's what we have, IRB or

00:45:12.116 --> 00:45:15.660
research permission for audio recording.

00:45:15.660 --> 00:45:18.840
There are also times where we use video
recording and we have a lot of video

00:45:18.840 --> 00:45:24.060
examples of students that you could see
in this course if you sign up for it.

00:45:25.150 --> 00:45:30.410
Video is a little bit more tricky as far
as students' reaction because they can

00:45:30.410 --> 00:45:35.950
visually see it more but
they still get used to it over time.

00:45:35.950 --> 00:45:39.700
If you start doing it when they're younger
I think it makes a big difference.

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:41.200
And then it's not up here but

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:46.760
I saw one that was kind of related
to it about is there any sort

00:45:46.760 --> 00:45:51.750
of app or have you had students use their
phones to record and they send it to you?

00:45:52.960 --> 00:45:55.530
Different educators have
done different things.

00:45:55.530 --> 00:45:59.080
We actually have a colleague who's
working on an App that we could actually

00:45:59.080 --> 00:45:59.950
all use together,

00:45:59.950 --> 00:46:05.080
it's not completed yet but, hopefully
in the next year or two it will be.

00:46:05.080 --> 00:46:09.560
But what most people have done now is
the teacher has either recorded using her

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:12.800
phone, if you have a smartphone,
it's very easy to do that.

00:46:12.800 --> 00:46:16.360
Just place the recording down on
the table in between the students.

00:46:16.360 --> 00:46:18.940
I haven't heard of the students
recording it and sending it to you, but

00:46:18.940 --> 00:46:22.690
I don't think there's any reason why they
couldn't if they're older obviously.

00:46:22.690 --> 00:46:24.700
I'm kind of old school and

00:46:24.700 --> 00:46:27.970
I actually have a digital recorder
that I use a lot of the time.

00:46:27.970 --> 00:46:33.410
I know some people have used iPads or
their actual computer.

00:46:33.410 --> 00:46:35.920
The bigger you get the bulkier
here it is obviously.

00:46:35.920 --> 00:46:38.680
But the most important thing
no matter what you use,

00:46:38.680 --> 00:46:41.630
is that you actually
can hear both of them.

00:46:41.630 --> 00:46:45.377
And so, if you have 20 conversations going
on in the room and you put the recorder

00:46:45.377 --> 00:46:49.203
on your desk, you're not really gonna hear
any of those conversations very well.

00:46:49.203 --> 00:46:52.550
So, you wanna it as close to
the students as you can get.

00:46:52.550 --> 00:46:53.730
Anyone else wanna and anything?

00:46:54.780 --> 00:46:59.187
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and more often than not
the students are more engaged in

00:46:59.187 --> 00:47:04.208
conversing because there's a little
bit more accountability to it.

00:47:04.208 --> 00:47:06.437
They're not freaked out.

00:47:09.126 --> 00:47:14.340
They're not freaked out as much as of
the video and so we've had kids with their

00:47:14.340 --> 00:47:19.639
phones and I actually call in and send
their files up to the teacher, name them.

00:47:19.639 --> 00:47:22.833
And so there's different ways to
do that but I would say overall,

00:47:22.833 --> 00:47:26.800
it's a good to get kids used to that and
recording it, even if you don't listen.

00:47:26.800 --> 00:47:29.370
Which you can't listen to all of them.

00:47:29.370 --> 00:47:32.410
It is a way of you might listen
to them and that's more so

00:47:32.410 --> 00:47:33.885
even if you're not recorded at all and

00:47:33.885 --> 00:47:36.430
two kids are in the corner talking
about the Friday night party.

00:47:36.430 --> 00:47:37.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:47:37.380 --> 00:47:41.875
&gt;&gt; So I would just say that,
as a quick answer.

00:47:41.875 --> 00:47:46.023
&gt;&gt; I would just add one more
thing around this recording

00:47:46.023 --> 00:47:49.614
of student conversation, is that working?

00:47:49.614 --> 00:47:54.079
Around recording a student conversation,
which is that,

00:47:54.079 --> 00:48:00.152
I know that many teachers around peer
observation of each other's teaching feel

00:48:00.152 --> 00:48:06.694
a little uncomfortable sometimes sharing
sort of observations of teacher practice.

00:48:06.694 --> 00:48:11.408
And that looking at each others
students language practice is,

00:48:11.408 --> 00:48:15.082
really lowers the threshold for
collaboration.

00:48:15.082 --> 00:48:19.511
That people feel much more comfortable
having their students being there.

00:48:19.511 --> 00:48:22.481
It's their students
behavior that's looked at.

00:48:22.481 --> 00:48:26.323
Even though it really is the teacher and
what the teacher does around good prompts

00:48:26.323 --> 00:48:29.600
and so forth,
that really supports a good conversation.

00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:33.900
But that this is a really good,
really good tool to embed within a teacher

00:48:33.900 --> 00:48:40.437
collaboration community at school
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:48:40.437 --> 00:48:42.540
that kind of leads us
into this next question.

00:48:42.540 --> 00:48:45.220
It sounds like that's a great piece
of advice you're giving teachers.

00:48:45.220 --> 00:48:47.500
But one of our participants is curious.

00:48:47.500 --> 00:48:51.500
What advice would you give for high school
teachers who are trying to increase and or

00:48:51.500 --> 00:48:55.140
improve the quantity and
quality of whole class conversations?

00:48:56.840 --> 00:48:59.710
&gt;&gt; Whole class.

00:48:59.710 --> 00:49:02.531
&gt;&gt; One thing that I would emphasize and

00:49:02.531 --> 00:49:06.393
have emphasized is building
some of the skills.

00:49:06.393 --> 00:49:09.640
The conversation skills that you
wanna see in paired conversations.

00:49:09.640 --> 00:49:14.097
Just having built that into
the whole class conversations, and

00:49:14.097 --> 00:49:18.070
having students take on some of the,
like I said before.

00:49:18.070 --> 00:49:19.940
A little more of that teacher like role.

00:49:19.940 --> 00:49:22.830
So, instead of,
when someone answers a question.

00:49:22.830 --> 00:49:25.410
Always the teacher's saying,
can you support that from the text?

00:49:26.440 --> 00:49:28.150
Pushing that out on the students and
saying.

00:49:29.940 --> 00:49:32.070
Okay, what do we say next?

00:49:32.070 --> 00:49:40.472
So really bring in the whole class kind
of habits of conversation out on to them.

00:49:40.472 --> 00:49:43.030
Because what they've been used to
especially before high school and

00:49:43.030 --> 00:49:44.275
that's a lot of years of school.

00:49:44.275 --> 00:49:48.255
They've been used to the teacher
directing all things conversation.

00:49:48.255 --> 00:49:51.065
And so changing that around and
saying, you know what?

00:49:51.065 --> 00:49:53.605
You guys are gonna do more of
the direction of the conversation and

00:49:53.605 --> 00:49:57.755
there's a few skills that you can work on,
and even as a whole class.

00:49:57.755 --> 00:50:01.170
For example,
can you support that idea from the text?

00:50:01.170 --> 00:50:02.740
Can you clarify that?

00:50:02.740 --> 00:50:04.470
What do you mean by this?

00:50:04.470 --> 00:50:08.630
Even those things that teachers usually do
are conversation skills that students can

00:50:08.630 --> 00:50:10.269
build in a whole class setting.

00:50:12.370 --> 00:50:17.223
In addition to that, another important
aspect is having a kind of clear prompt

00:50:17.223 --> 00:50:21.724
and direction for the classroom
conversation, which often doesn't.

00:50:21.724 --> 00:50:24.555
Sometimes it's just, okay I have
discussion down in my lesson plan and

00:50:24.555 --> 00:50:25.710
that's it.

00:50:25.710 --> 00:50:29.216
But if there isn't really
a firm direction that you have.

00:50:29.216 --> 00:50:34.000
For okay, what are we gonna do with this
five minute class, whole class discussion?

00:50:34.000 --> 00:50:36.480
You never know exactly
where you're gonna be.

00:50:36.480 --> 00:50:37.693
You can't script everything.

00:50:37.693 --> 00:50:41.760
You don't know exactly, but at least
you have at least a default direction,

00:50:41.760 --> 00:50:43.270
because you can go all over the place.

00:50:45.220 --> 00:50:45.870
Anybody else?

00:50:45.870 --> 00:50:48.110
&gt;&gt; I think that's a great answer.

00:50:49.630 --> 00:50:50.660
Excellent.

00:50:50.660 --> 00:50:54.620
Well our next question is, are there
differences of what to listen for

00:50:54.620 --> 00:50:59.400
in terms of analysis of conversations when
different disciplines ie., math, English,

00:50:59.400 --> 00:51:00.490
science art, etc.?

00:51:00.490 --> 00:51:05.468
Are there sources for good prompts for
distinct disciplines?

00:51:05.468 --> 00:51:07.032
&gt;&gt; It's a good question.

00:51:07.032 --> 00:51:11.130
&gt;&gt; It is a good question, and yes.

00:51:11.130 --> 00:51:12.820
I mean that the short answer is yes and
no.

00:51:12.820 --> 00:51:15.198
Could you put the question
right back up again?

00:51:15.198 --> 00:51:16.758
So I can see it.

00:51:18.480 --> 00:51:21.011
When we created this
conversation analysis tool.

00:51:21.011 --> 00:51:23.443
We did it as simple as we could.

00:51:23.443 --> 00:51:25.895
So that it would apply across disciplines.

00:51:25.895 --> 00:51:30.215
And so, conversing is an important
skill across all disciplines.

00:51:30.215 --> 00:51:35.425
So at the very basic level,
these three dimensions are going to

00:51:35.425 --> 00:51:40.085
be applicable in any grade level,
in any content area.

00:51:40.085 --> 00:51:45.960
But there is obviously going to
be difference in the qualitative

00:51:45.960 --> 00:51:50.740
nature of the conversations depending on
your grade level and on your content area.

00:51:50.740 --> 00:51:54.540
There's also gonna be great
differences and prompts.

00:51:54.540 --> 00:51:59.157
But as far as good source for
good prompts, in different disciplines.

00:51:59.157 --> 00:52:01.330
I think, and
Jeff may have more to add to this.

00:52:01.330 --> 00:52:06.291
But in my opinion,
the most important source for

00:52:06.291 --> 00:52:10.409
creating a good prompt is your content.

00:52:10.409 --> 00:52:15.359
So it's, you can't just say that
these are some good prompts for

00:52:15.359 --> 00:52:18.150
art or for environmental science.

00:52:18.150 --> 00:52:22.055
Because you're prompt passage connecting
very very intimately to the content that

00:52:22.055 --> 00:52:23.106
you're addressing.

00:52:23.106 --> 00:52:25.430
There are ways to phrase questions.

00:52:25.430 --> 00:52:28.700
So that they don't create
these one word answers, but

00:52:28.700 --> 00:52:33.547
if your prompt doesn't connect intimately
to the content you're addressing.

00:52:33.547 --> 00:52:35.990
Then it's not worth much anyway.

00:52:35.990 --> 00:52:39.195
Do you guys wanna add anything to it?

00:52:40.260 --> 00:52:43.571
&gt;&gt; One good example is that math
conversations are different from English

00:52:43.571 --> 00:52:44.406
conversations.

00:52:44.406 --> 00:52:46.482
And how are they different?

00:52:46.482 --> 00:52:51.656
For example,
one way of engaging in maybe you'd it call

00:52:51.656 --> 00:52:58.825
it supporting ideas in math is not by
referring to some example from the text.

00:52:58.825 --> 00:53:05.890
But rather using mathematical reasoning
based on mathematical principles.

00:53:05.890 --> 00:53:08.860
So you're actually not looking for,
well the teacher said this, or

00:53:08.860 --> 00:53:09.957
I found this in this book.

00:53:09.957 --> 00:53:12.020
You're actually looking for

00:53:12.020 --> 00:53:16.565
a mathematical explanation based
on mathematical principles.

00:53:16.565 --> 00:53:18.595
That's just one example
that might be different.

00:53:18.595 --> 00:53:22.747
Whereas in English, you're looking for
evidence from let's say the story, or

00:53:22.747 --> 00:53:27.167
in history might be looking at evidence
from a primary source document.

00:53:27.167 --> 00:53:30.880
And as Sara said, sources for
good prompts, yeah you can look.

00:53:30.880 --> 00:53:36.147
I mean I definitely encourage people
to look for good prompts, and look for

00:53:36.147 --> 00:53:41.095
and in some cases there will be good
prompts in curriculum materials.

00:53:41.095 --> 00:53:44.233
One in the area of essential questions for

00:53:44.233 --> 00:53:47.907
a unit that's often a decent
place to start, and

00:53:47.907 --> 00:53:53.820
that's kind of what Sara was saying too
is there's often this big question.

00:53:53.820 --> 00:53:57.080
Well that's something that we want
kids to be able to talk about.

00:53:57.080 --> 00:54:01.065
And so that would be another place at
least find what is the essential idea turn

00:54:01.065 --> 00:54:02.244
that into a question.

00:54:02.244 --> 00:54:07.630
And it's probably gonna be you
know fairly decent prompt overall.

00:54:07.630 --> 00:54:08.845
&gt;&gt; And this question of good prompts,

00:54:08.845 --> 00:54:13.045
I mean we're still doing research on it
right now because it's a hard question.

00:54:13.045 --> 00:54:16.325
And so sometimes you'll think
of a prompt is great and

00:54:16.325 --> 00:54:19.075
we'll think a prompt is great and
then it flops.

00:54:19.075 --> 00:54:22.565
And so it's a little bit of trial and
error to see whether or

00:54:22.565 --> 00:54:26.095
not a certain type of question
will lead to the conversation.

00:54:29.780 --> 00:54:33.500
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much Jeff,
Sara and Kenji for

00:54:33.500 --> 00:54:36.488
your time today and
everyone who joined us on the line.

